[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"SvelteKit Routing and Load Functions Tutorial"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to SvelteKit Routing"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"SvelteKit utilizes a filesystem-based routing system, meaning the structure of your application is defined by the directory layout inside the src/routes directory. Every +page.svelte file represents a route endpoint in your web application."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Dynamic and Optional Parameters"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can create dynamic routes by wrapping folder names in square brackets. For instance, creating a directory named src/routes/blog/[slug] will match URLs like /blog/sveltekit-routing."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[slug] - Matches a single path segment dynamically."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[...rest] - Rest parameters match any number of remaining segments."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Load Functions"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Load functions are used to fetch data before a page renders. They run on both the server and the client, depending on how they are configured. A load function is typically placed in a +page.js or +page.server.js file next to your +page.svelte."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"export async function load({ params }) {
  const { slug } = params;
  const response = await fetch("https://api.example.com/posts/" + slug);
  const post = await response.json();
  return {
    post
  };
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Consuming Data in Svelte Components"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The data returned by your load function is automatically passed to the corresponding +page.svelte component via the data prop. You can access it directly inside your markup."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  export let data;
</script>

<main>
  <h1>{data.post.title}</h1>
  <p>{data.post.content}</p>
</main>"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"+page.js vs +page.server.js"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"+page.js: Runs on both client and server. Useful for universal data fetching."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"+page.server.js: Runs exclusively on the server. Ideal for secure database queries and private environment variables."}]
